# The Python function needs backend/jobtracker (2.2 MB) — nothing else.
# Without these excludes the backend tree is ~880 MB and the function
# build exceeds Vercel's 250 MB unzipped limit.
backend/build
backend/dist
backend/checkpoints
backend/data
backend/tests
backend/.venv
backend/.venv311
backend/.pytest_cache
backend/.ruff_cache
backend/.mypy_cache
# NOTE: this file is read from the repository root for *every* Vercel project
# built from this repo, whatever its Root Directory. A blanket `apps` entry
# therefore deleted apps/web before the jobtracker-web build could run, and
# every push-triggered web build failed with NEXT_NO_VERSION. Exclude only the
# subtrees that are genuinely dead weight: apps/macos is 19.7 MB of Xcode
# project and image assets, apps/web is 2 MB and is the deployed web app.
apps/macos
apps/mobile
dist
docs
ml
scripts
# `.claude/` is untracked (see .gitignore) so it cannot exist in a git-triggered
# build — this line does nothing there. It is here for the CLI path, which is the
# break-glass route used when a deploy has to happen without a push, and which
# does NOT consult .gitignore. Locally `.claude/worktrees/` accumulates full
# checkouts of the repo: 29 GB across ~40 worktrees on 2026-08-14. A
# `vercel --prod` from the repository root therefore tries to enumerate all of it
# and dies with `Error: Request body too large. Limit: 10mb` — a file-count
# failure, not a byte-size one. That is exactly what stranded a rotated database
# password in the Vercel env while the running deployment kept the dead one, and
# took production down. Deploying from a clean worktree also avoids it; this line
# means you no longer have to remember to.
.claude
